"""World-transformation scenario fixtures — direct Python projection.

Manufactured by `ggen sync run` from `ontology/world-transformation-taxonomy.ttl`
via `queries/world-transformation/scenario_projection.rq`: one fixture
function per real `afl:WorldTransformationScenario` individual, each
returning a real, admitted `autofde_lab.powl.algebra.ChoiceGraph`:

    Start -> observe -> infer_desired_state -> compute_delta
          -> select_transformation -> manufacture -> verify -> End

This is the wider frame a scenario carries: current state + objectives +
constraints + authority -> inferred desired state -> semantic delta ->
candidate transformation -> manufactured system -> independent
verification. Debugging (the narrower `k8s_fault_universes.py` shape:
`observe -> hypothesize -> diagnose -> remediate`) is the special case
where the desired state is already known and the delta is a deviation to
restore.

Schema and worked-example fixtures only. `infer_desired_state`,
`compute_delta`, `select_transformation`, `manufacture`, and `verify` are
inert `Atom`s here, exactly like `remediate` already is in
`k8s_fault_universes.py` -- no live LLM inference, no delta computation, no
planner-portfolio wiring, no actuation. Additive only -- not wired into
`SreTroubleshootingDecisionBackend`, the breed ensemble, or the planner
federation, matching every other ggen-manufactured module in this repo.

Never reads, imports, or subprocesses `vendor/gyms/sregym` (see
`.claude/rules/gym-actuation-boundary.md`) -- every scenario's numbers are
authored directly in `ontology/world-transformation-taxonomy.ttl`.

GENERATED by ggen. DO NOT EDIT BY HAND -- edit
`ontology/world-transformation-taxonomy.ttl` and re-run `ggen sync run`
from the repo root to regenerate.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Callable

from autofde_lab.powl.algebra import (
    Atom,
    ChoiceGraph,
    ChoiceGraphEdge,
    End,
    NodeId,
    Start,
)

{% set scenario_rows = sparql_results | filter(attribute="rowKind", value="scenario") -%}
{% for srow in scenario_rows %}
